<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- Stays a leaf: folding into Humans.Email would close a project cycle: Humans.Email -> Humans.Campaigns -> Humans.Email. -->

  <!--
    Email's cross-section surface, carved into its own leaf project because most sections
    that do anything for a human tell them about it by mail: they build an EmailMessage in
    their own section's builder and send it through IEmailService. A section may not reference
    another section's implementation project, so putting these types in a Contracts/
    *folder* inside Humans.Email would leave every one of those callers with no way to name
    them (memory/architecture/section-project-cycle-fix.md — split the upstream section's
    contracts downward, never promote the shared type into Base). Shell consumes the leaf
    too, composing the HangfireImmediateOutboxProcessor -> IImmediateOutboxProcessor ->
    OutboxEmailService chain in EmailInfrastructureExtensions.

    Base is *not* among the consumers: its single ProjectReference is Humans.Users.Contracts
    and it names no type from this project.

    Everything with no consumer outside the section stays internal: the outbox interfaces
    (IEmailOutboxService, IEmailOutboxProcessor, IEmailOutboxRetention, IEmailOutboxManagement),
    the facilitated-message builder, the body composer, the SMTP
    transport, the repository and the entity.

    References Humans.Base only. Base references Humans.Users.Contracts, so nothing in that
    project may ever reference this one.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Humans.Base\Humans.Base.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="NodaTime" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
